VI  - 5
IP  - 1
DP  - 2008 Jan 1
TI  - Embryological origin of airway smooth muscle.
PG  - 4-10
AB  - Airway smooth muscle (SM) develops from local mesenchymal cells located around 
      the tips of growing epithelial buds. These cells gradually displace from distal 
      to proximal position alongside the bronchial tree, elongate, and begin to 
      synthesize SM-specific proteins. Mechanical tension (either generated by cell 
      spreading/elongation or stretch), as well as epithelial paracrine factors, 
      regulates the process of bronchial myogenesis. The specific roles of many of 
      these paracrine factors during normal lung development are currently unknown. It 
      is also unknown how and if mechanical and paracrine signals integrate into a 
      common myogenic pathway. Furthermore, as with vascular SM and other types of 
      visceral SM, we are just beginning to elucidate the intracellular signaling 
      pathways and the genetic program that controls lung myogenesis. Here we present 
      what we have learned so far about the embryogenesis of bronchial muscle.
