TI  - Autophagy in innate and adaptive immunity.
PG  - 22-8
LID - 10.1513/pats.200909-103JS [doi]
AB  - Autophagy (self-eating) is an evolutionary conserved simple process by which 
      cells target their own cellular organelles and long-lived proteins for 
      degradation. Recently, this simple ancient process has proved to be involved in 
      many biological aspects, including host defense, cell survival and death, innate 
      and adaptive immunity, and cancer. The implications of aberrant regulation of 
      autophagy in human diseases are just beginning to unravel. This is a brief review 
      of recent progress in the association of autophagy with innate and adaptive 
      immunity relevant to lung biology and disease.
