IS  - 1546-3222 (Linking)
VI  - 6
IP  - 3
DP  - 2009 May 1
TI  - Physiologic dysfunction of the asthmatic lung: what's going on down there, 
      anyway?
PG  - 306-11
LID - 10.1513/pats.200808-091RM [doi]
AB  - Asthma is a syndrome of lung dysfunction characterized by airflow obstruction, 
      reversibility to bronchodilators, and airways hyperresponsiveness (AHR). There is 
      a growing body of evidence that suggests that the principle defect in asthma is 
      the occlusion of the airway lumen by liquid, fibrin, and mucus. The fall in 
      FEV(1) observed in asthma is best explained by a loss of communicating airspaces 
      and the rise in residual lung volume. Imaging studies in both human patients and 
      experimental animals support this hypothesis. An increased propensity for the 
      airways to close can be a cause of AHR. We conclude that loss of lung volume 
      plays a central role in determining the dysfunction of the asthmatic lung as 
      measured by FEV(1). Together, these recent findings provide a better 
      understanding of the causes of airflow obstruction and AHR, suggesting new 
      avenues for the development of more effective asthma therapies.
