VI  - 3
IP  - 2
DP  - 2006 Apr
TI  - Immunoregulatory and antimicrobial effects of nitrogen oxides.
PG  - 161-5
AB  - The therapeutic effects of inhaled nitric oxide (NO) therapy are thought to be 
      restricted to the pulmonary vasculature because of rapid inactivation of NO by 
      hemoglobin in the bloodstream. However, recent data suggest that inhaled NO may 
      not only be scavenged by the heme iron of hemoglobin but also may react with 
      protein thiols in the bloodstream, including cysteine-93 of the hemoglobin B 
      subunit. Reaction of NO with protein or peptide thiols is termed S-nitrosylation 
      and results in the formation of relatively stable protein S-nitrosothiols that 
      carry NO bioactivity to distal organs. Thus, inhaled NO-induced protein 
      S-nitrosylation may allow inhaled NO to have multiple as yet undiscovered 
      physiologic and pathophysiologic effects outside of the lung. Here we review the 
      immunoregulatory and antimicrobial functions of NO and the potential effects of 
      inhaled NO therapy on host defense.
