DP  - 2011 Jun
TI  - HIV infection in the etiology of lung cancer: confounding, causality, and 
      consequences.
PG  - 326-32
LID - 10.1513/pats.201009-061WR [doi]
AB  - Persons infected with HIV have an elevated risk of lung cancer, but whether the 
      increase simply reflects a higher smoking prevalence continues to be debated. 
      This review summarizes existing data on the association of HIV infection and lung 
      cancer, with particular attention to study design and adjustment for cigarette 
      smoking. Potential mechanisms by which HIV infection may lead to lung cancer are 
      discussed. Finally, irrespective of causality and mechanisms, lung cancer 
      represents an important and growing problem confronting HIV-infected patients and 
      their providers. Substantial efforts are needed to promote smoking cessation and 
      to control lung cancer among HIV-infected populations.
