VI  - 6
IP  - 5
DP  - 2009 Aug 15
TI  - Imaging the molecular signatures of apoptosis and injury with radiolabeled 
      annexin V.
PG  - 469-76
LID - 10.1513/pats.200901-001AW [doi]
AB  - Annexin V is a ubiquitous intracellular protein in humans that has a variety of 
      intriguing characteristics, including a nanomolar affinity for the membrane-bound 
      constitutive anionic phospholipid known as phosphatidylserine (PS). PS is 
      selectively expressed on the surface of apoptotic or physiologically stressed 
      cells. As such, radiolabeled forms of annexin V have been used in both animal 
      models and human Phase I and Phase II trials to determine if this tracer can be 
      employed as an early surrogate marker of therapeutic efficacy in NSCLC and 
      non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. Many other pulmonary imaging applications of radiolabeled 
      annexin V are also possible, including the detection and monitoring of active 
      pulmonary inflammation and other pathophysiologic stressors in a variety of 
      diseases. In this article, the salient molecular features of apoptosis (and other 
      forms of cell death) that permits imaging with radiolabeled annexin V will be 
      discussed. The latest results from Phase II imaging trials with NSCLC and 
      non-Hodgkin's lymphoma will be also be detailed. Finally, the potential future 
      application of this tracer for the imaging of other pulmonary pathologies will be 
      outlined.
